          "question": "Which alarm features in reflux does guidance say should change the plan?",
          "answer": "The search results do not say which reflux alarm features should change the plan. None of the passages covers reflux directly. I can't fill that gap from memory, so please check a clinical guideline on reflux or ask a clinician.\n\nOne passage is relevant. The guidance on chest pain that may indicate a heart attack (red-flag-chest-pain) says that heartburn-like discomfort and cardiac pain can be hard to tell apart. It also says a history of reflux does not rule out a heart cause, and that relief from antacids does not rule it out either. It notes that breathlessness, unusual tiredness, nausea, or discomfort in the jaw, back, or upper abdomen can be signs of a heart attack, and that these are more often missed in women, older adults, and people with diabetes.\n\nIf someone has chest discomfort, pressure, or breathlessness, especially with the symptoms above, they should seek emergency care now. Do not wait to see whether antacids help.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
